  "query_preview": "..A ridge of high pressure will build to our east across \nthe high/southern plains through Monday as a strong low pressure \nmoves into the Pacific Northwest and an associated trough extends \ndown the West Coast. The upper low will move southeast into the \nwestern Great Basin by the middle of the week. The upper low then \nlifts northeast into the northern tier of states late in the week, \nwith weak troughiness persisting across the southwestern United \nStates.   \n\nMid level moisture will begin to move into the area Sunday afternoon \nfrom the south/southeast, as the ridge sets up to our east. This \nwill result in a very slight chance (5-10 percent) of showers and/or \nthunderstorms Sunday afternoon/evening to the south and east of \nTucson. Chances increase late Sunday night and Monday to the east of \nTucson, with lingering chances along the New Mexico state line into \nearly Tuesday. Most of this activity will be virga, although there \nwill be the potential for isolated 'dry' thunderstorms and the \nthreat of gusty outflow winds and lightning. Rainfall amounts will \nbe very light, generally only trace amounts to a couple of \nhundredths, with the highest precipitation totals forecasted in the \nWhite Mountains at 0.10\"-0.20\". High temperatures warm 1-3 degrees \non Sunday with highs topping out slightly above normal levels. \nReadings then lower back to slightly below normal levels Monday and \nTuesday. \n\nElevated southwest winds of 15-20 mph and gusts around 30 mph are \nexpected Monday and Tuesday across eastern Santa Cruz, Cochise, \nGraham and Greenlee counties. Breezy to windy southwest to west \nwinds then spread across much of southeast Arizona on Wednesday and \nThursday as the upper low moves closer to the region and the \ngradient tightens.  \n\nHigh temperatures Wednesday through Saturday of next week stay \nslightly below normal levels (1-4 degrees), with highs in the mid \n80s to around 90 degrees across the lower elevations of Santa Cruz \nand Cochise counties and in the lower 90s in the Gila River valley \nof Graham County and from Tucson westward.",
